Here's how to handle draft persistence with .NET MAUI's app lifecycle events:

**In your `App.xaml.cs`:**

```csharp
public partial class App : Application
{
    protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState? activationState)
    {
        var window = base.CreateWindow(activationState);

        window.Deactivated += (s, e) =>
        {
            // App going to background — save draft
            if (Shell.Current?.CurrentPage is NotePage page)
            {
                page.SaveDraft();
            }
        };

        window.Activated += (s, e) =>
        {
            // App returning to foreground — restore draft
            if (Shell.Current?.CurrentPage is NotePage page)
            {
                page.RestoreDraft();
            }
        };

        return window;
    }
}
```

**In your `NotePage.xaml.cs`:**

```csharp
public partial class NotePage : ContentPage
{
    private const string DraftKey = "note_draft";

    public void SaveDraft()
    {
        Preferences.Set(DraftKey, NoteEditor.Text ?? string.Empty);
    }

    public void RestoreDraft()
    {
        if (Preferences.ContainsKey(DraftKey))
        {
            NoteEditor.Text = Preferences.Get(DraftKey, string.Empty);
        }
    }

    // Clear draft when user explicitly saves
    private void OnSaveClicked(object sender, EventArgs e)
    {
        // ... your save logic ...
        Preferences.Remove(DraftKey);
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

- **`Window.Deactivated`** fires when the app loses focus (background).
- **`Window.Activated`** fires when the app regains focus.
- **`Preferences`** is ideal for small text — it persists across app restarts. For large drafts, use `FileSystem.AppDataDirectory` with file I/O instead.
- Clear the draft key after a deliberate save so stale data doesn't resurface.
